<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Hardened runtime is required for notarization and is enabled in
      tauri.conf.json. These entitlements re-open the specific holes the app
      genuinely needs. Nothing here is optional decoration.

      The three JIT/memory keys are what keep WKWebView alive. Without them the
      app launches fine in development and then crashes on launch AFTER
      notarization, which is the worst possible time to find out.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Microphone. Pairs with NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in Info.plist: the
      entitlement grants the capability, the plist string is what the user
      actually reads in the prompt. Both are required.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      NOT declared here, deliberately:

      - Screen recording has NO code-signing entitlement. It is purely TCC
        gated: the user enables the binary under System Settings > Privacy &
        Security > Screen & System Audio Recording. Note macOS Sequoia refuses
        ScreenCaptureKit to ad-hoc or unsigned binaries no matter what that
        pane says, so a real Developer ID signature is a hard requirement for
        Screen Sight and Guide Mode to work at all.
      - Accessibility (synthetic keystrokes) and Input Monitoring (the event
        tap) are likewise TCC grants, not entitlements.
      - com.apple.developer.persistent-content-capture, which would suppress
        Sequoia's recurring screen-recording re-prompt, is restricted by Apple
        to VNC apps and will not be granted here.
      - App Sandbox is absent on purpose. A global event tap, system-audio
        capture and cross-app accessibility are incompatible with it, which is
        also why this ships Developer ID direct rather than Mac App Store.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
